name: Bindings.Spec
---

# TypeScript bindings

`deus bindings` turns DEUS functions into a JavaScript module with a `.d.ts` that reads like a hand-written
library ([D81](../../docs/DECISIONS.md), [D87](../../docs/DECISIONS.md)). TypeScript calls DEUS through ordinary async
functions; DEUS calls TypeScript by performing an ability whose handler TypeScript supplies. The functions run
on the embeddable wasm kernel ([EMBEDDING.md](../../docs/spec/EMBEDDING.md)) in Node, a browser or a Worker.

This document is the spec, written first: requirements, then the API, then the mapping, then the theorems
that state the generator's laws. The theorems compile next to
[`deus/bindings.deus`](../../deus/bindings.deus), and `deus check Bindings.Spec` checks them. The end-to-end
test is [`src/bindings/bindings.test.ts`](../../src/bindings/bindings.test.ts), over
[`examples/geometry.deus`](../../examples/geometry.deus). Each requirement names what checks it.

## Extensions

| Term | URI |
| --- | --- |
| `req` | `org.dxos.deus.req@1.0` |

## Requirements

```mdl
req R-1: each exported DEUS function is a top-level `export declare function f(…): Promise<R>` in the `.d.ts`
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-2: every name the module emits is a valid JavaScript identifier or property name, whatever the DEUS name
  by: Bindings.Spec.identIsIdentifier | Bindings.Spec.identIsIdempotent | Bindings.Spec.propKeyIsPropertyName | Bindings.Spec.propKeyKeepsReserved
```

```mdl
req R-3: parameters keep their DEUS names; operation parameters, which have none, get distinct names by type
  by: Bindings.Spec.distinctIsUnique | Bindings.Spec.distinctKeepsUnique
```

```mdl
req R-4: a prefix selects every term under it; the rest of the name decides the TypeScript path
  by: Bindings.Spec.relativeNameStripsPrefix | Bindings.Spec.relativeNameSkipsOthers
```

```mdl
req R-5: the kernel applies an entry to one value, so only a function of exactly one parameter needs no adapter
  by: Bindings.Spec.adapterOnlyWhenNeeded | Bindings.Spec.entryIsFunctionWithoutAdapter | Bindings.adapterTakesNestedPairs
```

```mdl
req R-6: a variant type with fields gets a constructor object: `Shape.circle(2)` builds a `Shape`
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-7: values round-trip: what TypeScript passes decodes in DEUS as the same value, and back
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-8: the module works with no setup; `configure` sets module-level options, `load` makes a separate instance
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-9: `Throw` needs no handler: an unhandled `throw` rejects with `DeusThrow`, a defect with `DeusDefect`
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-10: a function that cannot be exported is listed with its reason, on stderr, in JSON and in the module
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

```mdl
req R-11: the output needs nothing from this package: the runtime and the kernel are copied next to the module
  by: vitest:src/bindings/bindings.test.ts > TypeScript calls DEUS functions and answers their abilities
```

## API

### Command

```text
deus bindings <prefix|name…> -o <dir> [--name <stem>]
```

| File                 | Content                                                                     |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `<stem>.js`          | the image, codecs, constructors, `configure`, `load` and one function per export |
| `<stem>.d.ts`        | types, constructor objects, handler interfaces, `Options` and the functions |
| `deus-runtime.js`    | a copy of `src/bindings/deus-runtime.js`: the kernel driver, codecs, `IO`   |
| `deus-runtime.d.ts`  | its declarations: `DeusDefect`, `DeusThrow`, `DeusDecodeError`, `IoHost`    |
| `deus_kernel.wasm`   | the embedding kernel, built with cargo when it is missing                   |

`<stem>` defaults to the first argument's last segment in lower case. A prefix selects every term under it;
a full name selects that term. Field accessors are not exported. The JSON document is in
[CLI-JSON.md](../../docs/spec/CLI-JSON.md).

In DEUS, the writer is `Bindings.generate : Text -> List Text -> Text -> {IO, Throw Text} (Text, Text, List Text, List (Text, Text))`:
the codebase directory, the prefixes and the command line give the `.js` text, the `.d.ts` text, the exported
names and the skipped names with reasons.

### The generated module

For `examples/geometry.deus` the `.d.ts` is, abridged:

```ts
export { DeusDecodeError, DeusDefect, DeusThrow, type Awaitable, type IoHost } from './deus-runtime.js';

/** A plane figure. */
export type Shape = { readonly $tag: 'circle'; readonly r: number } | { readonly $tag: 'rect'; readonly w: number; readonly h: number };
export type Tree<A> = { readonly $tag: 'leaf' } | { readonly $tag: 'node'; readonly left: Tree<A>; readonly value: A; readonly right: Tree<A> };
export type Point = { readonly x: number; readonly y: number };
export type Color = 'red' | 'green' | 'blue';

export interface LogHandler {
  info(text: string): Awaitable<void>;
}
export interface ThrowHandler {
  throw(text: string): Awaitable<never>;
}
export interface Handlers {
  readonly Throw?: ThrowHandler; // by default a throw rejects with DeusThrow
  readonly Log: LogHandler;
}
export interface Options {
  readonly wasm?: BufferSource | WebAssembly.Module; // by default deus_kernel.wasm next to the module
  readonly io?: IoHost;
  readonly handlers: Handlers; // optional when every handler is
}
export interface Geometry {
  readonly area: (s: Shape) => Promise<number>;
  // … one member per export
}

export declare function configure(options: Options): void;
export declare function load(options: Options): Promise<Geometry>;

/** Area, with pi taken as 3. */
export declare function area(s: Shape): Promise<number>;
export declare function total(shapes: ReadonlyArray<Shape>): Promise<number>;
// … one function per export

export declare const Shape: {
  readonly circle: (r: number) => Shape;
  readonly rect: (w: number, h: number) => Shape;
};
export declare const Tree: {
  readonly leaf: Tree<never>;
  readonly node: <A>(left: Tree<A>, value: A, right: Tree<A>) => Tree<A>;
};
```

Used from TypeScript:

```ts
import { area, configure, DeusThrow, ratio, Shape, total } from './out/geometry.js';

await area(Shape.circle(2)); // 12: the module loads deus_kernel.wasm next to it on first call
configure({ handlers: { Log: { info: (line) => console.log(line) } } });
await total([Shape.circle(2), Shape.rect(2, 3)]); // 18, after "summing 2 shapes"
await ratio(1, 0); // rejects with DeusThrow('division by zero')
```

- The module-level functions share one instance, loaded on the first call with the options `configure` last
  set; `configure` drops the instance, so the next call loads a new one. A failed load is not cached.
- `load(options)` returns an object with the same functions on an instance of its own.
- A function under a nested name (`Geometry.Shapes.scale` for prefix `Geometry`) is a member of a constant named
  after the path (`Shapes.scale`); a constant that is also a type's constructor object holds both.
- A handler that is missing is reported when the DEUS code first performs its ability, naming the option.
- Each call takes an idle kernel instance or instantiates one, so calls may overlap and a handler may call
  back into the module.

Not exported, each with its reason:

- a polymorphic function (a type variable in its signature);
- a parameter or result of function type;
- `Dict`, `Ref`, `MutMap` and other builtin types without a JSON form;
- `Option` of `Unit` or of `Option`, whose `none` and `some undefined` would both be `undefined`;
- a function that performs an ability at different type arguments from an earlier export;
- a name the module already uses: `load`, `configure`, the error classes, a constructor object, or a clash
  between a function and a path.

## Mapping

The TypeScript type of each DEUS type, and the JSON its codec reads and writes:

| DEUS                        | TypeScript                                         | JSON                                      |
| --------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------- |
| `Int`                       | `number` (32-bit; encoding truncates with `\| 0`)  | a number                                  |
| `Text`                      | `string`                                           | a string                                  |
| `Bool`                      | `boolean`                                          | `true` / `false`                          |
| `Unit`                      | `void`; a `Unit` parameter is left out             | `null`                                    |
| `List a`                    | `ReadonlyArray<A>`                                 | an array                                  |
| `Option a`                  | `A \| undefined`                                   | `#Option`, tag 0 or 1                     |
| `(a, b)`, `(a, b, c)`       | `readonly [A, B]`, `readonly [A, B, C]`            | right-nested `#Pair`s                     |
| `structure P where x : X …` | `type P = { readonly x: X; … }`                    | `{"$": hash, "tag": 0, "fields": [x, …]}` |
| `inductive T` with fields   | `{ readonly $tag: 'ctor'; readonly f: F; … } \| …` | `{"$": hash, "tag": n, "fields": […]}`    |
| `inductive T`, all nullary  | `'red' \| 'green' \| …`                            | `{"$": hash, "tag": n, "fields": []}`     |
| `T a` (a type parameter)    | `T<A>`; the codec is `T$(codecA)`                  | as above                                  |

- A field without a name is `_0`, `_1`, …. A structure's fields come from its namespace entry, an inductive
  type's from its `fields` metadata.
- An identifier has other characters replaced by `_` and a keyword takes a trailing `_` (`ident`). A property
  name (an operation, a constructor) may be a keyword, so only the characters are replaced (`propKey`):
  `Throw.throw` is `throw`. A type's TypeScript name is its last segment, or its whole name with `.` as `_`
  when two types share the last segment.
- Values are written with the type's hash as `$`, which the kernel accepts without an IRI form; values read
  are matched by tag against the static type, so `$` is not checked.
- A tuple of four or more items keeps its nesting after the third: `(a, b, c, d)` is `readonly [A, B, readonly [C, D]]`.
- Module-internal names in the `.js` start with `$`, which no DEUS name produces, so no export can shadow them.

### Abilities

Every ability in the exported functions' effect rows, except the builtins, gets a handler interface with one
method per operation. A parameterised ability is typed at the arguments the functions perform it at (`Throw
Text` gives `throw(text: string): Awaitable<never>`). An operation whose result is a type variable of its own
(`throw : e -> a`) returns `never`: its handler must throw, and the call rejects with what it threw. Operation
parameters are named after their types (`text`, `n`, `flag`, `items`, `maybe`, `pair`, or the type's name),
numbered where they repeat. Requests are dispatched by the ability's IRI and, as a fallback, by its hash.

`IO` is answered by the runtime: `print`, `eprint`, `time`, `getEnv` and `wallClock`. Any other `IO`
operation fails the call; files and processes are not available to embedded code.

### Entries

The kernel applies an entry to exactly one value. A function of one parameter is its own entry. For a
constant, or a function of `n ≥ 2` parameters, the image holds an adapter definition, named by the hash of its
`(term …)` form:

```text
(lam (ref "f"))                                                         ; a constant
(lam (match (var 0)                                                     ; n = 3
  (case (pcon "#Pair" 0 (pvar) (pcon "#Pair" 0 (pvar) (pvar)))
    (app (ref "f") (var 2) (var 1) (var 0)))))
```

The module passes the arguments as the right-nested pair the adapter matches (`$rt.args`). The image is the
closure of the exported functions plus the adapters, sorted by hash, with the IRI forms of the types and
abilities they use and a `(main …)` naming the first export's entry.

## Theorems

The laws of the generator, stated over `deus/bindings.deus`. The oracles here are written apart from the
implementation, so a theorem cannot pass by restating the code.

The laws are the definitions under `Bindings.Spec`: `deus ls Bindings.Spec.` lists them and `deus view` prints them.

`adapterTakesNestedPairs` in `deus/bindings.deus` pins the exact adapter for three parameters.
